    "q-223474": {
      "dom_id": "step-q223474",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "A sequence is defined as $f(n)={n}^{2}-1$ for $n\\ge 1.$ What are the $3$rd and $6$th terms of the sequence?\n\nThe $3$rd term is\n{{field-1}}\nand the $6$th term is\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Using the definition of the sequence, we find the $3$rd and $6$th terms corresponding to $n=3$ and $n=6,$ respectively:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ={3}^{2}-1=8 \\\\ f(6) & ={6}^{2}-1=35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $3$rd term is $8$ and the $6$th term is $35.$",
